Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the 'ability to consummate' concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: 'Company Material Adverse Effect' means any event, condition, change, effect, or circumstance (each, an 'Effect') that, individually or in the aggregate with other Effects, (a) has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the business, financial condition, or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole, or (b) would, or would reasonably be expected to, prevent, materially delay, or materially impair the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement. However, in the case of clause (a) only, the following will not be deemed to constitute a Company Material Adverse Effect: (i) changes in general economic conditions in the United States or any other country; (ii) changes in conditions in the financial markets, credit markets, or capital markets; (iii) changes in the industries in which the Company and its Subsidiaries operate; (iv) changes in regulatory or political conditions; (v) acts of terrorism or war; (vi) natural disasters or pandemics (including COVID-19); (vii) the announcement or pendency of this Agreement; (viii) actions taken with Parent's consent; (ix) changes in GAAP or law; (x) changes in stock price or trading volume; (xi) failure to meet projections; (xii) Transaction Litigation. Provided further, that with respect to clauses (i) through (iv) and (vi), such Effects will be considered only if they disproportionately affect the Company compared to others in the industry. (Pages 12-13)
