Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the \u201cability to consummate\u201d concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: \u201cMaterial Adverse Effect\u201d means any change, effect, event, or circumstance that, individually or in the aggregate with other changes, effects, events, or circumstances, has a material adverse effect on (i) the business, financial condition, or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole, or (ii) the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement; provided, however, that, for purposes of clause (i) only, none of the following shall be deemed to be or constitute a Material Adverse Effect: (A) changes in general economic or political conditions or changes in the financial markets in general; (B) changes in laws, regulations, or accounting standards; (C) acts of war, terrorism, or natural disasters; (D) any change in the Company\u2019s stock price or trading volume; (E) any failure by the Company to meet projections or forecasts; (F) any actions taken by the Company at the express written request of the Parent; provided further, that with respect to clauses (A) through (C), such changes shall be taken into account if they have a disproportionate impact on the Company compared to other companies in the same industry. Importantly, the ability to consummate the transactions is not subject to the carveouts listed in (A) through (F). (Pages 15-16)\nAnswer:
Choices:

